[{:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-in-the-world"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"we need a system for our own agents and it's simpler to say this is a base world you can get (I suppose a custom one should be allowed if for good reason) and customize and then it'll run fast and we can optimize for one happy path\"; \"I think my.test is better\" (the API is the agent's, beside my.task and my.agent). And: \"Shrink the tests down to sizes that capture the error class they are looking for ... seon agents [should] have tests that run over a configured time ... rejected\".
What exists: seon.test-store/test-db (the test store's seed as a value) and seon.test-store/test-branch! (a copy-on-write branch of it, deleted after), used by 19 test namespaces; the seed is seon.db/seed-tx: 18 namespaces of 20 one-line (+ x i) functions and one task each in a dead attribute (:task/name), no wiki, no tests, no agents, no errors. So a test about the wiki, problems, an agent's record or a render imports the real program instead: measured 2026-10-30, 90 of 1,022 in-process tests run over 2e8 ticks, 236 s of the in-process suite's 354 s, the top 18 over 1e9 (seon.vector-test/the-pyramid-ranks-as-an-exact-search 8 G, seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current 5.3 G, the whole-src imports of seon.source-test and the whole-program health of seon.problems-test), and every src change reran the whole-program ones (an incremental prove of one function: 131 s, ten of them 43 s).
The world: an in-memory db value with Seon's schema, built once per process from fixtures (measured: ~0.6 M ticks, ~13 ms; no store), holding a small slice shaped like production (AGENTS.md section 5: sample data looks like production data): a few namespaces with real references between vars, a macro, contracts, a test namespace with deftests; a few wiki pages with sections and citations to those vars; tasks in today's schema with plans and proofs; an agent with its branch, turns, evals and recorded errors. Sized so a test over it runs well under the ceiling. It replaces seon.db/seed-tx's example program as the test store's seed.
The API, my.test (src/my/test.clj, the agent's, documented in docs/ with the testing section): (my.test/world): the base as a db value, for a test that only reads (its fixture applied with d/db-with, no write); (my.test/in-world fixture f): f run on a copy-on-write branch of the world with fixture (tx-data) transacted, the branch deleted after, whatever f does. A custom world is a named declaration with its reason, built and kept the same way; nothing else makes a store.
Then: the 90 over-ceiling tests move onto the world (each keeps its claim; a cost proof keeps the size where its growth shows and drops the rest; a test that scanned all of src or the wiki takes a fixture; the context session reviews the diff of its 27 in problems, render, render-cost, context, context-cost and ctx), and :seon.config.agent/test-ticks drops from 1e10 to 2e8, a test past it red, its error naming my.test/world and the shrink.
Deletes: seon.db/seed-tx's example program (replaced by the world), seon.test-store/test-db and test-branch! once their callers use my.test (or they become the two my.test calls)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{my.test-test/the-world-is-built-once-per-process
                      my.test-test/a-fixture-adds-to-the-world-and-leaves-it-as-it-was
                      my.test-test/a-world-test-runs-far-under-the-ceiling
                      my.test-test/the-pages-example-runs}
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
